WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.294
C strings are null-terminated sequences in
memory.

2
00:00:03.374 --> 00:00:07.691
A function that expects a C string
typically scans until it reaches a terminator.

3
00:00:07.771 --> 00:00:11.010
If the expected terminator is missing
inside valid storage,

4
00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.757
the function contract has already been
violated.

5
00:00:13.837 --> 00:00:15.551
A safer API may reduce this risk,

6
00:00:15.631 --> 00:00:19.822
but it cannot repair an input that does
not meet its documented preconditions.

7
00:00:19.902 --> 00:00:22.886
String length and buffer capacity are
different facts.

8
00:00:22.966 --> 00:00:25.823
Length is the current content before the
terminator.

9
00:00:25.903 --> 00:00:29.967
Capacity is the storage available for
content and any required terminator.

10
00:00:30.047 --> 00:00:32.142
A call that writes data needs capacity.

11
00:00:32.222 --> 00:00:36.540
A call that reads data may need length,
termination, or both depending on the API.

12
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.414
Reviewers should not let those terms blur
together.

13
00:00:39.494 --> 00:00:42.414
Pointer lifetime and storage mutability
also matter.

14
00:00:42.494 --> 00:00:46.558
A pointer to stack storage, heap storage,
static storage, a string literal,

15
00:00:46.638 --> 00:00:50.003
or a slice inside another buffer can have
very different rules.

16
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:51.671
Some storage can be modified.

17
00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.719
Some should be treated as read-only.

18
00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:57.482
Some is valid only until a function
returns or a dynamic string grows.

19
00:00:57.562 --> 00:00:59.911
Binary data is not automatically a C
string.

20
00:00:59.991 --> 00:01:03.420
A buffer may contain embedded null bytes,
may not be terminated,

21
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.802
or may use an encoding that the string API
does not understand.

22
00:01:06.882 --> 00:01:10.691
Treating arbitrary bytes as text can make
length checks, comparisons,

23
00:01:10.771 --> 00:01:12.740
formatting, and logging incorrect.

24
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.931
Use byte-oriented APIs when the data is
not a valid string.

25
00:01:16.011 --> 00:01:17.979
Every string API assumes a contract.

26
00:01:18.059 --> 00:01:20.409
Some require valid null-terminated input.

27
00:01:20.489 --> 00:01:22.139
Some accept explicit lengths.

28
00:01:22.219 --> 00:01:25.204
Some always terminate output when capacity
is nonzero.

29
00:01:25.284 --> 00:01:27.823
Some report truncation through a return
value.

30
00:01:27.903 --> 00:01:28.729
Some allocate.

31
00:01:28.809 --> 00:01:29.507
Some borrow.

32
00:01:29.587 --> 00:01:33.397
A safer API is still unsafe when the
caller violates those assumptions.

33
00:01:33.477 --> 00:01:37.604
The practical review habit is to name the
representation before choosing the

34
00:01:37.684 --> 00:01:38.256
function.

35
00:01:38.336 --> 00:01:42.336
Is this a C string, a bounded byte range,
a dynamic string object, a builder,

36
00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:43.495
or an encoded field?

37
00:01:43.575 --> 00:01:48.020
Once the representation is clear, the
correct API contract is much easier to check.
